\f0\fs24 \cf0 Note that the firewall daemon, firewalld, on Manjaro, blocks multicast; something on OpenBSD is interfering with mDNS but not multicast, since logging works.\
\
If avahi can't be made to work on OpenBSD, consider using multicast to manage the Registry announcements - a service looking to the Registry can send a multicast request, waiting on a UDP port for a response and timing out if no response is received. Avahi sort-of works, except it seems to stop refreshing it's cache - it doesn't show services on the same machine, let alone all the services on the network.\
For the Registry, if it sees a request it responds with the TCP/IP port information, much as the mDNS response contains.\
For the Registry, it initially asks if there's a Registry present and quits if it finds one; otherwise it sets up a listen thread to handle the multicast requests.\
\
Rough logic flow, non-Registry service:\
1. Launch a thread to catch posts from the Registry multicast port.\
2. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
3a. If there is no response in a reasonable time, report that there is no Registry.\
3b. If there is a response, record the connection details. [Note that this is like the effect of the mDNS code.]\
\
Rough logic flow, Registry service:\
1. Start the TCP/IP listener.\
2. Launch a thread to catch posts from the Registry multicast port, marking it as 'watching for another Registry'.\
3. Send a request to the Registry multicast port, asking for the TCP/IP connection information.\
\pard\tx566\tx1133\tx1700\tx2267\tx2834\tx3401\tx3968\tx4535\tx5102\tx5669\tx6236\tx6803\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 4a. If there is no response in a reasonable time, switch the thread to 'watching for a request'.\
4b. If there is a response, exit due to the existence of a running Registry.\
5. If a request arrives on the Registry multicast port, respond with the details of the TCP/IP listener.\
\
Note that this is similarly structured to the mDNS code, but is simpler since very little needs to be transferred.\
